                     UPGRADING TO MODTRAN3.5

    MODTRAN3.5 contains a number of major and minor upgrades from MODTRAN3.  For
MODTRAN runs not involving clouds, backward compatibility has been maintained in
that all MODTRAN3 tape5 input files with input variable ICLD (CARD2) equal to
zero are acceptable MODTRAN3.5 input files (for ICLD>0 see A below).  MODTRAN3.5
upgrades and extensions to the MODTRAN3 input stream (A, J, M, N, P, and Q) are
described below.  A complete users manual for MODTRAN3.5 is in preparation.  For
questions and/or comments about MODTRAN, please send email to Gail Anderson
(ganderson@plh.af.mil) or Alexander Berk (lex@spectral.com).

(A) CLOUD/RAIN MODELS
    The MODTRAN layer cloud/rain models have been made easier to modify and more
flexible.  Clouds can be placed anywhere within the defined atmosphere, can
co-exist with aerosols, and can have a mixed phase composition.  A complete
description is in the CLOUD file on the Phillips Laboratory ftp site [Berk, A.
(1995), Upgrades to the MODTRAN Layer Cloud/Rain Models, Rpt. No. SSI-SR-56,
Spectral Sciences, Inc., 99 S. Bedford St., Burlington, MA, 01803].
    MODTRAN3 tape5 input files with ICLD>0 can only be run using MODTRAN3.5
after minor modification.  For CIRRUS clouds (ICLD=18, 19, or 20), the CARD2A
inputs CTHIK, CALT, CEXT and ISEED must be entered using FORMAT(3F8.3,I4);
MODTRAN3 uses FORMAT(3F10.3,I10).  MODTRAN default water clouds and rain models
(ICLD=1-10) can be run using MODTRAN3.5, but require a CARD2A with 11 inputs
[FORMAT(3F8.3,2I4,6F8.3)].  The default values can be obtained by setting each
input to negative nine.  Thus, to convert a MODTRAN3 tape5 with ICLD=1-10, add
the following CARD2A:
  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9  -9  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000  -9.000

(B) HITRAN96 DATABASE
    The molecular band model parameter files (MOLBMP96.ASC and CFCBMP96.ASC)
were generated using the HITRAN96 database [Rothman, L. S. (1996), personal
communications].

(C) LORENTZ-HALF WIDTHS
    Based on a review of the HITRAN96 database, all Lorentz half-widths are now
modeled to vary with temperature to the minus three-quarters power.

(D) BAND MODEL PARAMETER TEMPERATURE GRID
    The MODTRAN3.5 band model parameters are tabulated at six temperatures:
180, 205, 230, 255, 280 and 305K.  The lower minimum temperature (previously
200K) enables the tropopause at the Antarctic to be modeled.

(E) LINE TAIL BAND MODEL PARAMETERS
    Improved line tail absorption coefficients have been calculated by more
carefully accounting for location of line centers.  A companion refinement was
made to the equivalent width calculation within MODTRAN.

(F) ASCII BAND MODEL PARAMETER FILE
    The format of the ASCII band model parameter file was changed to accommodate
up to 11 temperatures within 132 columns.  The 1 cm-1 molecular band model
parameters are listed on pairs of lines [FORMAT(I6,I5,1P11E11.3,/6X,I5,11E11.3)].
The first line contains the frequency [cm-1], a molecular label [1=H2O, 2=CO2,
3=O3, 4=N2O, 5=CO, 6=CH4, 7=O2, 8=NO, 9=SO2, 10=NO2, 11=NH3, 12=HNO3], and
absorption coefficients [cm-1 / atm] at the 6 temperatures.  The second line
contains the Lorentz half-width [cm-1] at 273.15K (times 10000) and line spacing
band model parameters [cm] at the 6 temperatures.  For line tails (out to
25cm-1), the first line contains the frequency [cm-1] and both lines contain a
molecular label (offset by 12, e.g. 13=H2O, 14=CO2, ...) and absorption
coefficients.

(G) BINARY BAND MODEL PARAMETER FILE
    Each record in the binary direct-access molecular band model parameter file
contains a single set of band model data (2 lines from the ASCII file).  The
only exception is the first record which contains the number of temperatures (6)
and their values (180, 205, 230, 255, 280 and 305K), the band model spectral bin
width (1 cm-1), the first frequency (0 cm-1) and the last frequency (22681 cm-1)
in the file.

(H) THE "PARAM.LST" FILE
    The list of parameters in the PARAM.LST (formerly parameter.list) file has
been increased, and each of the parameters has been defined.

(I) CORRELATED-K APPROACH
    The next generation of MODTRAN, MODTRAN4, will calculate multiple scattering
using a Correlated-k (CK) approach.  Some of the hooks to CK are already in
MODTRAN3.5.  The PARAM.LST parameter KNTRVL, the number of K sub-intervals, has
been set to 1.  Calls to CK routines have been commented out by replacing
columns 1-4 with the string "CORK".

(J) VERTICAL FLUXES AT THE PATH FINAL ALTITUDE
    When the input variable IMULT (CARD 1) is set to 1, vertical fluxes are
calculated at the location of the observer, H1.  In MODTRAN3.5, IMULT can
alternatively be set to -1 to calculate vertical fluxes at H2.  Generally, H2 is
the path final altitude.  However, for a path to space (ITYPE=3) in which H2 is
specified, H2 is the tangent height; if IMULT is set to -1 with this geometry
specification, then the fluxes will be calculated at the tangent point.

(K) SOLAR VERTICAL FLUX CALCULATIONS
    The solar vertical flux calculations using the two-stream method have been
refined in an attempt to improve low sun angle calculations.  Key inputs to the
modification are the transmitted solar irradiance to the bottom and top of each
layer.  The ratio of these irradiances (effectively the decrease in solar
transmittance across a layer) can sometimes exceed the calculated vertical layer
transmittance.  When this occurs, the solar vertical fluxes are calculated using
the older approach and a warning is written to the tape8 output file.  This
warning does not mean that the calculations are invalid!

(L) TWO-STREAM MULTIPLE SCATTERING RADIANCES
    In its calculation of multiple scattering (MS) radiances, MODTRAN3.5 makes 
an initial attempt to incorporate the variation of vertical fluxes within
atmospheric layers by applying the "linear-in-tau" approach for thermal emitted
radiance to the MS terms.

(M) ALTITUDE GRID
    Three new layer boundary altitudes have been added to the MODTRAN model
atmospheres at 55, 60 and 80 km.  Also, the MODTRAN ground altitude input GNDALT
(CARD 2) can now be negative.

(N) SATELLITE OBSERVATIONS OF SOLAR REFLECTIONS
    When the observer altitude H1 is above the top of the defined atmosphere,
MODTRAN lowers H1 to the top of the atmosphere and adjusts the path zenith angle
appropriately.  MODTRAN3.5 now corrects the solar geometry too.

(O) OUTPUT STREAMS
    A number of changes were made to the MODTRAN output files.  Incremental
(path within a single layer) column densities for each extinction source is
written to tape6; no output extends beyond 132 columns; and the multiple
scattering contribution to the thermal radiance is now printed.

(P) PLOT OUTPUT FILE
    MODTRAN3.5 allows the user to create a plot (x vs y) output file, "plot.dat".
This file is defined using three new CARD 4 inputs, YFLAG, XFLAG and DLIMIT

    READ(IPR,'(4I10,2A1,A8)')IV1,IV2,IDV,IFWHM,YFLAG,XFLAG,DLIMIT

    If YFLAG is 'T' or 't', transmittances are output;
    If YFLAG is 'R' or 'r', radiances are output (irradiances if IEMSCT=3);
    Otherwise, no plot.dat file is created.

    If XFLAG is 'M' or 'm', wavelengths are in um and radiances are in W SR-1 CM-2 / um;
    If XFLAG is 'N' or 'n', wavelengths are in NM and radiances are in uW SR-1 CM-2 / NM;
    Otherwise, frequencies are in CM-1 and radiances are in W SR-1 CM-2 / CM-1.

    DLIMIT is the delimiter written between MODTRAN runs (IRPT>0).

If DLIMIT is blank, no separation is inserted between MODTRAN runs.  If a
delimiter is used, then the minimum and maximum transmittance/radiance is
written on the delimiter line.

(Q) SOLAR SPECTRUM
    The use of the solar irradiance function inputs SUN1 and ISUN (CARD1A) has
been modified.

    If SUN1 is .FALSE.,
         solar irradiances are obtained from a block data containing
         KURUCZ data degraded to 5 cm-1 (FWHM triangular slit) resolution
	 and tabulated each cm-1.

    If SUN1 is .TRUE.,
         KURUCZ data from the file sun_kur.dat (formerly "sun2") is
         degraded to |ISUN| cm-1 (FWHM triangular slit) resolution and
         tabulated each cm-1.  If ISUN is negative, the resulting data
	 is output to file "sun####.dat", where #### is the resolution.
